You can achieve this by creating a `<div>` element that contains both the input field and the SVG icon. Here's an example code snippet to demonstrate how you can achieve this:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Search Input with SVG Icon</title>
<style>
  .input-container {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    width: 300px;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    border-radius: 5px;
    padding: 5px;
  }

  .input-field {
    flex: 1;
    padding: 5px;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 5px;
    outline: none;
  }

  .icon {
    fill: #333;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="input-container">
  <input type="text" class="input-field" placeholder="Type something here">
  <svg class="icon" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" onClick="search()">
    <path d="M8 1l7 7-7 7v-5.176a6 6 0 1 1 -.625.625h.001zm10.968 8.676a8 8 0 1 0 -.687.719l4.415 4.414-1.414 1.414-4.421-4.413a8 8 0 0 0 -3.293-13.121c-1.34 0 -2.592.329 -3.712.904a3 3 0 1 0 4.116 4.357c.36.36.83.586 1.342.758l4.413 4.421z"/>
  </svg>
</div>

<script>
  function search() {
    const input = document.querySelector('.input-field');
    const value = input.value;

    // Do something with the value
    console.log(value);
  }
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

In the above code, there is an input field inside a container div along with a search icon (SVG element). The `onClick` event is added to the SVG icon, calling the `search()` function, which retrieves the input value and logs it to the console. You can modify the `search()` function to perform the desired action or call another function when the icon is clicked after entering text in the input field.

